well let's speak now to Mandu Reed who's  the leader of the women's equality party  and she was at the Buckingham Palace  reception and witnessed the incident so  Maddie Reed thanks so much for talking  to us this afternoon so you were there  tell us what what you saw  yeah um we arrived at the reception and  were ushered into the main room where  the reception was taking place myself  and gozi and another campaigner were  standing together three of us black  women we were approached by a member of  the royal household who came over began  the usual small talk very quickly she  zoomed in on ngozi and asked her where  she was from and goes he said she was  from Sister space which is the  organization she leads but what followed  was almost an interrogation there was  question after question about where she  was really from where her people came  from what her nationality was and and  goes he said I'm British I was born in  the UK but that wasn't enough for this  person this person really wanted to  extract some other information about her  Heritage her African Heritage and it was  really uncomfortable in the end it was a  prolonged encounter over several minutes  and in the end it left you know all of  us feeling like perhaps we didn't belong  um at the event perhaps we weren't truly  welcome well yes and go see herself  described it as a violation didn't she  that's left her feeling stunned so how  did it leave you feeling listening to it  you know  um I also felt stunned I didn't know how  to respond  um I found myself thinking for sure if  ngozi was a white woman there's no way  that line of of questioning would have  taken place and it's not what you expect  we were invited guests to the really  important reception about a really  important subject ending violence  against women and girls we weren't gate  crashes but we were sort of made to feel  almost like trespassers and it really  took the Shine off what should have been  an important event to celebrate people  doing work that really makes a  difference in the world and raising  awareness about a crucial issue but  instead at least for the three of us who  were there in that encounter we were  left wondering if actually uh we were  kind of wanted or welcomed well yes and  just in the last few hours Buckingham  Palace have put out a statement haven't  they and they've said  um that the member of Staff concerned  has stepped aside and has offered her  profound apologies they also go on the  palace to say that they take the  incident extremely seriously they've  described the comments as unacceptable  and deeply regrettable are you reassured  At All by that reaction  you know not really because  actually I don't want to see her step  down I want to see her step up I want to  see all of them step up I want to see  the Royal household as a whole  acknowledge  um that institutional racism is part of  the culture and I want to see them  tackle that head on I I think zooming in  on this one individual doesn't really  bring any comfort it reminds me of the  whole bad apples narrative it reminds me  of the whole okay let's treat this as an  isolated incident and move on from it  approach to dealing with these issues  and and I think what's needed is  something bigger something more  substantive and something that uh really  meets the scale of of how these  incidents play out what they represent  and what they mean for people in this  country like myself like and gozi who do  belong here who are British  um but aren't necessarily received as  such and and when you say you think  there's an issue of institutional racism  when you hear their Buckingham Palace  also saying that all members of the  household are being reminded of  diversity and inclusivity policies does  that go any way towards meeting your  concerns  it's important that they've got policies  it's important that people are reminded  of them but what's more important is the  culture within the institution what's  more important is what people Experience  day to day we have heard Meghan markle's  accounts her and Harry's accounts of of  some of the things they were on the  receiving end of the experience we had  yesterday you know in a way validated  those and it was just one afternoon  reception we weren't having to  experience that day in day out so sure  policies are important but what's more  important is leadership what's more  important is acknowledging the breadth  and depth of work that needs to be done  and I think ngozi herself and her  organization have a lot of experience to  offer here they they do cultural  competence training for the Met police  for example to help them understand how  better to deal with black women who are  survivors of domestic abuse I think the  Royal household need to acknowledge that  perhaps in introducing that sort of  training  would be a meaningful first step towards  taking their institution their  organization on a journey to handling  these kind of things a whole lot better
 

